<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kostrzyn nad Odrą</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Küstrin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kostrzyn nad Odrą, in German Küstrin, is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> joins the Oder. Margrave John of Brandenburg-Küstrin made it his capital and a powerful fortress in the 16th century, and in 1730 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> was imprisoned there. The old town was destroyed in 1945, and the town then became Polish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra.jpg" alt="Kostrzyn nad Odrą" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kapitel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostrzyn_nad_Odra_dron_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town lies in the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a> at the confluence of the Oder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> rivers, about 90 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>. Until 1945 the municipal area also included the suburb of Küstrin-Kietz on the west bank of the Oder, which now belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as part of the Küstriner Vorland municipality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was held by the Piast dukes and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from about 960 until 1261. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> used it as a staging area before the Battle of Cedynia in 972. In 1223 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Władysław Odonic</a> of Greater Poland granted the fortress to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, and the name first appears in 1232, as Cozsterine. In 1261 the Ascanian margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> seized it for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a>, and by 1300 it held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> town rights and grew through river trade. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a> in 1373, then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, which sold it to Brandenburg after the Thirteen Years' War began in 1454.</p><p>The castle built by John of Brandenburg-Küstrin grew into one of the largest fortresses of the region, though its expansion limited the room for the town. The fortress surrendered to the French without a fight in 1806, and the town burnt to the ground in 1814 during the French retreat. About 640 insurgents of the Greater Poland Uprising were held there in 1848. Railways reached it in 1857 and 1875, making it an important rail hub. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the fortress held a prisoner-of-war camp for Allied officers, regarded as the only German camp of that war from which no one escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town housed a prisoner-of-war camp and a sub-camp of Sachsenhausen. Air raids and the fighting on the Oder destroyed almost 95 per cent of its buildings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured it on 11 March 1945. The Potsdam Conference gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the remaining Germans were expelled and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> resettled the ruins. The remnants of the old town were razed and their bricks used to rebuild other Polish cities. The central area now lies around the railway station, east of the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kostrzyn nad Odrą” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Black Reichswehr, a secret reserve organised as labour battalions, was set up in 1921 and dissolved in 1923 after its members attempted the <b>Küstrin</b> Putsch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He was kept at <b>Küstrin</b> to study statecraft and administration.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-18">18 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1535 his elder son Joachim II became elector of the Margraviate of Brandenburg, while the younger, John, received the Neumark east of the Oder, with Crossen and other districts, ruling from <b>Küstrin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-brandenburg-kustrin">Margraviate of Brandenburg-Küstrin</a></li></ul></section>
